              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your patient, a 71-year-old female, admitted on 18/01/1959 with acute right lower quadrant abdominal pain, nausea, and low-grade fever, clinically suspicious for appendicitis. On admission, physical examination revealed marked tenderness at McBurney's point with signs of peritoneal irritation. Blood tests confirmed leukocytosis with left shift. Abdominal ultrasonography supported the diagnosis of acute appendicitis. Given her age and clinical presentation, a laparoscopic emergency appendectomy was performed on 19/01/1959 without intraoperative complications. Postoperatively, the patient received intravenous fluids, analgesics, and antibiotic therapy. Wound healing was satisfactory, vital signs remained stable throughout, and she mobilised well with physiotherapy support. She was discharged home on 20/01/1959 in good general condition. Follow-up at your practice in two weeks is recommended. Please do not hesitate to contact me with any queries.</div>"
            },
            "code": {
